<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Rochelle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Rochelle is a port city on the Atlantic coast of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in the old province of Aunis. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it exported wine and salt to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and passed between the English and French crowns, falling to Louis VIII in 1224. It later became a Huguenot stronghold, taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a> after the siege of 1627–1628, and a base of French trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-rochelle.jpg" alt="La Rochelle" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mister Brown · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_rochelle,_Le_vieux_port.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
